EXAMPLES=[]
def ex(n,problem,steps,answer,pages):
 EXAMPLES.append({'id':'we-4-source-'+str(n),'num':str(n),'title':'Example '+str(n),'problem':problem,'given':'Expansion about the stated point.','method':'Evaluate derivatives at the expansion point and insert their Taylor coefficients.','steps':steps,'answer':answer,'progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'pageStart':pages[0],'pageEnd':pages[-1],'verification':'complete source question and proof compared with JPG/PDF'}]})
ex(8,'Use the fourth-order Taylor polynomial of eˣ about 0 to estimate f(0.2).',['Every derivative of eˣ equals eˣ, so the values through fourth order at zero are all 1.','Insert these values into p₄(x) = Σ from k = 0 to 4 of f⁽ᵏ⁾(0)xᵏ/k!.','The constant and linear terms are 1 + x; the remaining terms are x²/2 + x³/6 + x⁴/24.','At x = 0.2 these contributions are 1,0.2,0.02,0.0013333333 and 0.0000666667.','Their sum is exactly 1.2214 for the given decimal x.','Check against e⁰·² ≈ 1.22140275816. The absolute error is about 2.75816 × 10⁻⁶.','The source equality is correct after rounding to four decimal places; the finite polynomial does not equal the exponential exactly.'],'p₄(0.2) = 1.2214; absolute error ≈ 0.00000275816.',[110])
ex(9,'Use Taylor’s series for f(x) = eˣ about x₀ = 2.',['Put h = x − 2.','All derivatives of eˣ equal eˣ, so every derivative at 2 equals e².','The constant is e²; the linear contribution is e²h.','The quadratic and cubic contributions are e²h²/2 and e²h³/6.','Thus eˣ = e²[1 + h + h²/2! + h³/3! + …].','This agrees independently with eˣ = e²eʰ, and the exponential series converges for every real h.','The source uses 7.3891 for e²; that coefficient is rounded. Use the exact e² for accurate computation.'],r'\(e^x=e^2[1+(x-2)+(x-2)^2/2!+(x-2)^3/3!+...]\).',[112])
ex(10,'Use Maclaurin’s series for f(x) = aˣ about 0.',['For a real exponential, require a > 0. Set L = ln a.','Rewrite aˣ as exp(Lx).','Each derivative multiplies by L: f⁽ⁿ⁾(x) = Lⁿaˣ.','At zero, f⁽ⁿ⁾(0) = Lⁿ.','Insert the constant 1, linear Lx, quadratic L²x²/2! and cubic L³x³/3!.','The full series is Σ (Lx)ⁿ/n!, converging for every real x.','If a = 1, L = 0 and the series is the constant 1, as expected.'],r'\(a^x=1+x\ln a+x^2(\ln a)^2/2!+x^3(\ln a)^3/3!+...\).',[112])
ex(11,'Use Maclaurin’s series for f(x) = eˣ about 0.',['Every derivative of eˣ is eˣ.','At zero all derivative values are 1.','Taylor coefficient for xⁿ is therefore 1/n!.','The constant and linear terms are 1 + x.','Add x²/2! + x³/3! and the continuing higher terms.','The ratio of successive absolute terms is |x|/(n + 1), approaching zero for each fixed x.','Thus the infinite series equals eˣ for all real x; a finite truncation is an approximation.'],r'\(e^x=1+x+x^2/2!+x^3/3!+...\).',[112])
ex(12,'Use Maclaurin’s series for sinx about 0.',['The derivatives cycle sinx,cosx,−sinx,−cosx.','At zero their values cycle 0,1,0,−1.','Even powers have zero coefficients.','The linear coefficient is 1; the cubic is −1/3!.','The next nonzero coefficients are +1/5! and −1/7!.','Hence sinx = x − x³/3! + x⁵/5! − x⁷/7! + …, for all real x.','All powers are odd, consistent with the function being odd; use radians in these derivatives.'],r'\(\sin x=x-x^3/3!+x^5/5!-x^7/7!+...\).',[112])
ex(13,'Use Maclaurin’s series for cosx about 0.',['The derivatives cycle cosx,−sinx,−cosx,sinx.','At zero their values cycle 1,0,−1,0.','Odd coefficients vanish.','The constant is 1 and quadratic coefficient is −1/2!.','The quartic is +1/4! and sixth-degree coefficient is −1/6!.','Thus cosx = 1 − x²/2! + x⁴/4! − x⁶/6! + …, for every real x.','The alternating even powers agree with cosine being even, and termwise differentiation gives the negative sine series.'],r'\(\cos x=1-x^2/2!+x^4/4!-x^6/6!+...\).',[113])
ex(14,'Use Maclaurin’s series for tanx about 0.',['The value tan0 is 0 and the first derivative sec²0 is 1.','The second derivative 2sec²x tanx vanishes at zero.','The third derivative 2sec⁴x + 4tan²x sec²x equals 2 at zero.','Insert these values: tanx = x + 2x³/3! + … = x + x³/3 + … .','To check the next coefficient, use T′ = 1 + T². With T = x + Ax³ + Bx⁵, compare 3A = 1 and 5B = 2A.','This gives A = 1/3,B = 2/15. Thus the next term is 2x⁵/15.','The nearest poles to zero are ±π/2; the Maclaurin series applies for |x| < π/2, not for all real x.'],r'\(\tan x=x+x^3/3+2x^5/15+...\), |x| < π/2.',[113])
ex(15,'Use Maclaurin’s series for logₐ(1 + x) about 0.',['Require a > 0,a ≠ 1 and x > −1.','Change base: logₐ(1 + x) = ln(1 + x)/ln a.','The first three derivatives at zero are 1/ln a,−1/ln a and 2/ln a.','Insert them: logₐ(1 + x) = [x − x²/2 + x³/3 − …]/ln a.','The general nth coefficient is (−1)^(n−1)/(n ln a) for n ≥ 1.','The source factor logₐe equals 1/ln a, so its formula is equivalent.','The series converges for −1 < x ≤ 1; at x = 1 it is the alternating harmonic series divided by ln a.'],r'\(\log_a(1+x)=\frac1{\ln a}(x-x^2/2+x^3/3-...)\).',[113,114])
ex(16,'Use Maclaurin’s series for ln(1 + x) about 0.',['The value at zero is ln1 = 0.','First derivative is 1/(1 + x), giving value 1 at zero.','Second derivative is −1/(1 + x)², giving −1 at zero.','Third derivative is 2/(1 + x)³, giving 2 at zero; the source formula omits the numerator 2.','Insert the coefficients: x − x²/2! + 2x³/3! + … = x − x²/2 + x³/3 − … .','For n ≥ 1 the coefficient is (−1)^(n−1)/n.','The real function requires x > −1, while the series converges for −1 < x ≤ 1. A finite sum is an approximation.'],r'\(\ln(1+x)=x-x^2/2+x^3/3-x^4/4+...\).',[114])
ex(17,'Use Maple taylor for eˣ to order 4 and sinx to order 5 about zero.',['For eˣ, all derivatives at zero equal 1.','The terms below degree 4 are 1 + x + x²/2 + x³/6, followed by O(x⁴).','The source command is taylor(e^x,x=0,4); it displays ln(e) coefficients. These equal 1 if e is Euler’s constant.','For sinx, the derivatives at zero give only the nonzero x and −x³/6 terms below degree 5.','The source command taylor(sin(x),x=0,5); displays x − x³/6 + O(x⁵).','The number 5 here is the cutoff order, not five nonzero terms. To show five nonzero sine terms, one would need degrees through 9.','The Context Menu alternatives series(e^x,x,4) and series(sin(x),x,5) are preserved beside the printed outputs. These results are independently checked series, not an executed Maple session.'],r'\(e^x=1+x+x^2/2+x^3/6+O(x^4)\); \(\sin x=x-x^3/6+O(x^5)\).',[114])
CHALLENGE={'id':'ex-4-taylor-challenge','qNo':'Challenge','question':'Use Taylor’s theorem to compute series at x₀ = 3 for sinx,cosx,tanx,aˣ,eˣ,logₐ(1 + x),ln(1 + x).','steps':[
 'Put h = x − 3. Taylor expansion about 3 uses f(3) + f′(3)h + f″(3)h²/2! + f‴(3)h³/3! + … .',
 r'(i) By sin(3+h) = sin3 cosh + cos3 sinh, where cosh and sinh here mean cos(h) and sin(h), obtain sin3 + h cos3 − h²sin3/2 − h³cos3/6 + … . The infinite series converges for all real h.',
 r'(ii) cos(3+h) = cos3 − h sin3 − h²cos3/2 + h³sin3/6 + …, all real h.',
 r'(iii) Set T = tan3,Q = sec²3. The first three derivatives at 3 are Q,2QT and 2Q² + 4QT². Thus tan(3+h) = T + Qh + QTh² + Q(1 + 3T²)h³/3 + … . Its interval is |h| < 3 − π/2, the distance to the nearest tangent pole.',
 r'(iv) With a > 0,L = ln a, a^(3+h) = a³[1 + Lh + L²h²/2 + L³h³/6 + …], all real h.',
 r'(v) e^(3+h) = e³[1 + h + h²/2 + h³/6 + …], all real h.',
 r'(vi) For a > 0,a ≠ 1: logₐ(4+h) = logₐ4 + [h/4 − h²/32 + h³/192 − …]/ln a. The series converges for −4 < h ≤ 4, with h > −4 required by the real logarithm.',
 r'(vii) ln(4+h) = ln4 + h/4 − h²/32 + h³/192 − …, since ln(4+h) = ln4 + ln(1+h/4). The same interval −4 < h ≤ 4 applies.',
 'For sine and cosine, h is an angle in radians. The nearest-singularity restriction is necessary for tangent and logarithms; listing derivative coefficients alone does not justify all-real convergence.'
 ],'answer':'The seven Taylor expansions about 3 and their convergence intervals are shown above.','progressiveSolution':True,'solutionOrigin':'SpaceBook learning enhancement','sourceReferences':[{'page':114,'jpg':'061_page_2.jpg','verification':'all seven source challenge functions visually compared with JPG/PDF'}]}
CHALLENGE['steps'][1]='(i) Use sin(3 + h) = sin3 cos(h) + cos3 sin(h). Insert the sine/cosine Maclaurin series to obtain sin3 + h cos3 − h²sin3/2 − h³cos3/6 + … . The infinite series converges for all real h.'
CHALLENGE['solution']='\n'.join(CHALLENGE['steps'])
